\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 A collection of pure 
\f2\fs26 bash
\f1\fs24  alternatives to external processes and programs. The 
\f2\fs26 bash
\f1\fs24  scripting language is more powerful than people realise and most tasks can be accomplished without depending on external programs.\
Calling an external process in 
\f2\fs26 bash
\f1\fs24  is expensive and excessive use will cause a noticeable slowdown. Scripts and programs written using built-in methods (
\f3\i where applicable
\f1\i0 ) will be faster, require fewer dependencies and afford a better understanding of the language itself.\
The contents of this book provide a reference for solving problems encountered when writing programs and scripts in 
\f2\fs26 bash
\f1\fs24 . Examples are in function formats showcasing how to incorporate these solutions into code.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 STRINGS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf0 Trim leading and trailing white-space from string\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 This is an alternative to 
\f2\fs26 sed
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 awk
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 perl
\f1\fs24  and other tools. The function below works by finding all leading and trailing white-space and removing it from the start and end of the string. The 
\f2\fs26 :
\f1\fs24  built-in is used in place of a temporary variable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Example Function:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 trim_string() \{\
    # Usage: trim_string "   example   string    "\
    : "$\{1#"$\{1%%[![:space:]]*\}"\}"\
    : "$\{_%"$\{_##*[![:space:]]\}"\}"\
    printf '%s\\n' "$_"\
\}\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Example Usage:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 $ trim_string "    Hello,  World    "\
Hello,  World\
\
$ name="   John Black  "\
$ trim_string "$name"\
John Black\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Trim all white-space from string and truncate spaces\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 This is an alternative to 
\f2\fs26 sed
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 awk
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 perl
\f1\fs24  and other tools. The function below works by abusing word splitting to create a new string without leading/trailing white-space and with truncated spaces.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Example Function:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 # shellcheck disable=SC2086,SC2048\
trim_all() \{\
    # Usage: trim_all "   example   string    "\
    set -f\
    set -- $*\
    printf '%s\\n' "$*"\
    set +f\
\}\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Example Usage:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 $ trim_all "    Hello,    World    "\
Hello, World\
\
$ name="   John   Black  is     my    name.    "\
$ trim_all "$name"\
John Black is my name.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

