\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Use 
\f2\fs26 #!/usr/bin/env bash
\f1\fs24  instead of 
\f2\fs26 #!/bin/bash
\f1\fs24 .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The former searches the user's 
\f2\fs26 PATH
\f1\fs24  to find the 
\f2\fs26 bash
\f1\fs24  binary.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The latter assumes it is always installed to 
\f2\fs26 /bin/
\f1\fs24  which can cause issues.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 NOTE
\f1\b0 : There are times when one may have a good reason for using 
\f2\fs26 #!/bin/bash
\f1\fs24  or another direct path to the binary.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 # Right:\
\
    #!/usr/bin/env bash\
\
